1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
Intermediate code is machine-independent. Machine code is not.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Three jobs: select instructions, allocate registers, clean up locally.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
This page computes four counts: instructions, registers, live variables, colours.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
Syllabus position: Section 7 names the inputs, not these three jobs by name.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Five statements, but only four different computations.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
A DAG node is a value; an edge is a use. Shared nodes are shared values.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
Six leaf occurrences become four loads; five operators become four.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
The DAG is built from the block, so it can never disagree with it.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
Same DAG, two machines: 8 instructions at 3 registers, or 12 at 1.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Selection is a covering problem: which instruction covers which nodes.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
Both programs were executed: they agree on every input tried.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
Fewer instructions usually means more registers. That is the trade.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
(a-1)*(((b+c)/3)+d): the numbering gives 3, the machine needs 2.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
The formula charges each constant a register. The instruction set does not.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Two registers, eight instructions, no spill — the 2017 answer.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
The formula is exact when every leaf costs a register; the search is exact always.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
A variable is live at a point if some path from there reads it first.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
The worst point holds 4 variables: a, d, e and x at the if.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
y is never live: it is written and then never read.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
Maximum live is a lower bound on registers, never an upper bound.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
One node per variable, one edge per pair that is alive together.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
Degrees: a has 5, d and e have 4, c and x have 3, b has 1.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
Every edge has a witness point; every non-edge has none.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
Interference is about overlap, not about being mentioned together.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
A colour is a register. Adjacent nodes must get different colours.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Greedy colouring, highest degree first, uses 4 colours here.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
The clique {a, c, d, e} is 4 nodes all joined, so 3 colours is impossible.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
A valid colouring is a proof of sufficiency. A clique is a proof of necessity.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
Four colours become R1 to R4, and every variable gets its register.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
GATE CSE 2013 Q49 asks for the minimum with no spill: the answer is 4.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
With only 3 registers one variable must spill: the cheapest is d, at cost 3.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
Spilling a variable outside every clique cannot lower the answer.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
A peephole pass looks at a few instructions at a time and rewrites them.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
Five rules remove five instructions: 11 become 6.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
Both programs were run on 25 input pairs and agreed on memory and registers.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
Each rule needs a condition; drop the condition and the rule is wrong.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
First check: does the question ask for registers, variables, or instructions?

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Second check: is code motion allowed, and are spills allowed?

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
For an expression tree, ask whether the constants are immediates before using the formula.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Two checks, then the arithmetic: that order removes most wrong options.

